I work on the platform that lets customers and support agents manage their software subscriptions — things like updating billing details, cancelling, or toggling auto-renewal. The same subscription can be modified from two places: a customer self-service portal and an internal agent console.

The interesting challenge is that any change has to be applied by a downstream system of record that we integrate with asynchronously, and we have to prevent two conflicting changes to the same subscription at once — from either channel. So a lot of the design is about coordinating concurrent updates across systems we don't fully control."

At a high level: reads go through a GraphQL gateway backed by Elasticsearch. A write goes through a rules engine that decides whether the action is even allowed, then to a write service that publishes the change to the downstream system over Kafka and places a lock on that subscription. When the downstream system finishes and publishes an acknowledgment back, a consumer service releases the lock. So it's an asynchronous, event-driven update with a locking scheme layered on top for concurrency control."

That's the shape of it — I'm happy to go deeper on the concurrency model, the async event flow, or the failure modes, whichever is most useful.




