Sure. I work on the backend services behind a subscription management platform — the system customers use to manage software subscriptions: updating billing details, cancelling, toggling auto-renewal, that kind of thing.
What makes it architecturally interesting is that two different clients consume the same backend APIs — a customer-facing self-service portal, and an internal console that our support agents use. So the same subscription can be modified from either channel. And on top of that, changes aren't applied by us directly — they're processed by a downstream system of record that we integrate with asynchronously over Kafka.
So the core challenge is concurrency control: making sure two conflicting updates to the same subscription — from either channel — can't race each other while a change is still in flight downstream. We handle that with a locking mechanism layered over the async flow.
